{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786510221,"nonce":"WfiG6EwcPlGmfm9yahdwh7c8SBI=","target":"e3bb3fddce63082c2bc91293f7bf30d7a0388e8f10ce7ee129aa0e492cd8aa21","message":"Title: The label typeahead scans the whole tenant's `alerts` per keystroke, and its own comment says no index serves it\n\n**What is wrong.** The code states the defect and then dismisses it on a premise that does not hold. `internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:932-937`:\n\n\u003e *NOTE (planner): no index serves this. alerts_labels_gin is jsonb_path_ops, which supports containment only; key ENUMERATION is a scan of the org's alerts. Aggregating adds no scan — the rows were already being read to be DISTINCTed. It is a discovery endpoint, not a hot path, and it is bounded by `limit`; an expression index over jsonb_object_keys would be a migration this module does not own.*\n\nTwo of those clauses are wrong. `LIMIT` bounds rows **returned**, not rows read — the `GROUP BY … ORDER BY n DESC` above it must aggregate the whole tenant before it can know which twenty-five rows to return. And the endpoint is the alert filter bar, which is used during an incident.\n\n`distinctLabelNamesSQL`, `alert.go:916-922`, expands every non-synthetic alert in the org through a `LATERAL jsonb_object_keys` before grouping:\n\n```\nSELECT k, count(*) AS n\n  FROM alerts a, LATERAL jsonb_object_keys(a.labels) AS k\n WHERE a.org_id = $1 AND NOT a.synthetic AND ($2 = '' OR k LIKE $2 || '%')\n GROUP BY k\n ORDER BY n DESC, k ASC\n LIMIT $3\n```\n\n`distinctLabelValuesSQL`, `:952-961`, is the same shape without the lateral, and its note at `:966-967` calls it *\"a bounded scan\"*. The `alerts_labels_gin` index it names is indeed containment-only — `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:92`, `USING GIN (labels jsonb_path_ops)` — so neither query has a driving index.\n\n**The demonstration.** Both are request-path reads: `GET /api/v1/labels` (`internal/alerts/api/handlers.go:723-724`) and `GET /api/v1/labels/{name}/values` (`:750-751`), registered at `internal/alerts/api/router.go:115`, reached through `service/read.go:432` and `:439`. A typeahead fires per keystroke, and the `LIKE $2 || '%'` prefix filter is applied to the lateral output, after expansion — so a longer prefix narrows the result and not the work.\n\n`alerts` is on ADR 0024's never-reaped list (`docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:39`): one row per alert identity, for the life of the install. One alert may carry up to `MaxLabels = 64` labels (`internal/alerts/domain/labels.go:22`), enforced as bound B3. So the lateral expansion is one to sixty-four rows per alert. At 200 000 alerts averaging ten labels each, one keystroke aggregates about 2M intermediate rows to return at most 200 (`ClampLimit`'s ceiling, `internal/platform/db/limits.go:26`); at the enforced ceiling it is 12.8M. That is per keystroke, per operator, on the filter bar of an incident view — the moment the comment's \"not a hot path\" stops being true.\n\n**Why it matters.** The clamp that is supposed to prevent this is documented as doing exactly what it cannot do here — `internal/platform/db/limits.go:33-35`: *\"A List that forgets to clamp is a List that asks Postgres for an unbounded scan, so this is applied at every List in every repository.\"* These two queries clamp and still scan, because the cost is in the aggregation upstream of the clamp. The comment's own escape hatch, *\"a migration this module does not own\"*, is an ownership statement rather than a technical obstacle.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/repository/alert.go:916-922` (`distinctLabelNamesSQL`), `:932-937` (the note that admits it), `:952-961` and `:966-967` (the value typeahead and its note), `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:92` (`alerts_labels_gin`, containment only), `internal/alerts/api/handlers.go:723-724,750-751` (the routes), `internal/alerts/domain/labels.go:22` (`MaxLabels`).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** An expression index over `jsonb_object_keys(labels)` serves the name enumeration and the value typeahead has a driving index of its own, or both read a maintained label-cardinality projection; the planner notes at `alert.go:932-937` and `:966-967` are rewritten to name the index that serves them.","files":null}]}